tax  the  rich  sometimes a few simple words is all it  takes to set things off we cannot afford  to continue this level of income and  wealth inequality do you want a symbolic  high tax rate on on the high income  people to win votes politically or do  you really want more tax revenue coming  into the government good times are to  their benefit crises are even more to  their benefit they get further and  further and further ahead i'm just  asking you to practice what you preach  let me ask you one other question here i  do practice what i treat well you don't  i'm calling people  the government you're not tension  between wealthy elites and everyone else  is certainly nothing new but in the  2020s with a widening income gap rising  inflation high gas prices astronomical  home values offshore tax havens the  billionaire space race and a shrinking  middle class the question of what  governments should do about the  ultra-rich has become a central one in  economic policy  recently  president joe biden pitched a two-fold  solution for america  the first part is a minimum tax rate of  at least 20 percent on the wealthiest  americans on their full income including  unrealized appreciation of assets or  capital gains  taxing unrealized appreciation is  important to remember since capital  gains are normally taxed after you sell  an asset and collect a profit the second  part is a separate tax at death on most  previously untaxed gains from  appreciated assets even if the assets  are not sold our income tax  is progressive that as you make more  income  you pay higher taxes higher tax rates  however  our income tax  fails to pick up  all of what people might think of as  income  and our income tax system does not tax  appreciation as you go along  although biden's plan has been dubbed  the billionaire tax it actually targets  households with a net worth of 100  million dollars or more so what are some  of the arguments for and against this  idea let's start with the ones in favor  billionaires often pay lower tax rates  than the average american warren buffett  once famously pointed this out comparing  his own rate to his secretary debbie  bosonic  in 2010 buffett's income tax rate was  17.4 percent  while bosonics was 35.8 percent i think  it should be a defining issue debbie  works just as hard as i do and she pays  at twice the rate i pay i think that's  outrageous the biden white house  looked at the forbes 400 wealthiest  households  and they determined that if you counted  unrealized gain as income the effective  tax rate  by these 400 households  was about 8  and the effective tax rate  for a typical worker in the u.s  income tax rate is closer to 22  billionaires can also borrow money  against their accumulated gains on  assets without triggering capital gains  this allows individuals to not declare  income and not be obligated to pay  income taxes  elon musk is a good example of this  a propublica investigation showed  between 2014 and 2018  musk's wealth grew by 13.8 billion  dollars but he paid 455 million dollars  in income taxes during that same period  in 2018 he paid zero federal income  taxes but in december 2021 in a twitter  clapback to senator elizabeth warren  musk claimed he would be paying more  taxes than any american in history that  year  he later tweeted his tab was above 11  billion dollars  the bill was so big because he exercised  millions of stock options which are  taxed at a high ordinary tax rate if the  white house were able to regularly claim  taxes from billionaires they believe the  deficit would be reduced by 360 billion  dollars in the next 10 years  the plan would also tax billionaires  annually as they go just like everyone  else  finally collecting annual taxes on  unrealized gains would reduce the  ability of the ultra-rich to hide money  in tax shelters or to pass down assets  to errors with gains never being taxed  these assets can be passed on to their  ears  with what is known as a step up in basis  and the ears take  the assets  at their full fair market value and this  massive amount  of appreciation  that potentially should be taxed  vanishes now for some of the arguments  against the proposal  first calculating how to tax unsold  assets is very complicated especially  when some assets such as fine art are  hard to value and publicly traded stocks  can go down as well as up if we rely on  a deemed sale and not a true sale well  we have no sales price  as reference  we have to guess what the assets going  to be worth if there were a sale  second when you lack an actual sale  the taxpayer who is deemed to make the  sale  might not have the dollars to pay the  tax  and so that imposes a liquidity  barrier to trying to squeeze out  taxes from someone who hasn't sold any  now the biden billionaire tax  tries to make allowances for both of  these  uh difficulties  the american tax system is already very  complicated and difficult to administer  adding another level of bureaucracy to  calculate these new taxes could make  things even more inefficient biden's tax  would target the biggest job and wealth  creators in america this could then  affect their ability to promote more  economic and technological growth again  elon musk is cited as an example to  support this argument he took the excess  capital from creating a brand new  industry in electric cars and went into  space in a way that the government never  could he beat them at their own game we  need people like that finally the  ability for the federal government to  tax property is constitutionally  questionable as this is typically the  realm of state and local taxes the u.s  constitution  limits the type of taxes our federal  government can impose  and it's unclear whether biden's  billionaire tax  can fall within the safe harbor of our  income tax or whether it would be viewed  as a tax on wealth or property  and that generally cannot be done under  our constitution  as for whether or not this proposed tax  will be enacted the chances are slim  democratic senator joe manchin whose  vote would be needed to pass the  billionaire plan  has already said he will not support  taxing unrealized gains  however even if the plan does not go  ahead the notion that the ultra-rich  have to pay their fair share is a  message we'll likely keep hearing on the  campaign trail heading into the midterm  elections  [Music]  you

